.TH TGLFS-CONVERT-WEBM 1 "October 2026" "tglfs 0.1.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tglfs-convert-webm \- create a separate encrypted WebM preview copy without temporary video files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tglfs convert-webm
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fIufid\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
Reads an owned MP4, MOV, M4V, MKV or WebM through bounded decrypt/decompress streams. FFmpeg encodes VP9 video and optional Opus audio, and the generated bytes flow directly into a new encrypted upload. No plaintext source, generated video, pass log, or sample clip is written to disk. The original file card and chunks remain unchanged.
.PP
The new copy uses the source password, including an empty password, with a fresh salt and authenticated encryption nonces. New copies use file-card protocol v5, media-preview v1 and an encrypted media-index v1. Complete current-cipher v1, v2, v3 and non-archive v5 source cards are accepted; archive v4 and v5 and unknown future versions are refused. There is no migration or overwrite.
.PP
Requires FFmpeg 8 or newer with libvpx-vp9, libopus, SSIM, loopback decoders and bounded filter buffers, plus matching ffprobe. Capabilities are checked before downloading source bytes. A metadata prefix of at most 64 MiB is inspected first, then the source is streamed from the start for conversion and complete UFID verification. No seekable input or temporary-file fallback is available. MP4/MOV movie metadata must precede media data. Files with unsuitable interleaving or excessive metadata/buffering fail closed.
.PP
Preserves video resolution and variable frame timing. Accepts progressive, opaque, 8-bit 4:2:0 SDR video with square pixels, no rotation, one video track and at most one mono/stereo audio track. HDR, alpha, interlacing, unsupported timing, extra tracks and unsupported audio are refused. Maximum dimensions are 4096 per side and 4096 by 2304 pixels per frame. Audio is encoded to Opus at 48 kHz, 128 kbit/s mono or 192 kbit/s stereo. The encrypted seek index stores duration and fragment locations so TGLFS Preview can scrub without downloading preceding media.
.SH QUALITY
Decodes the actual encoded video with a loopback decoder and compares full-resolution luma in beginning, middle and end clips of up to one second each. Each clip requires SSIM of at least 0.98 before the complete file card is published. Failed or missing comparisons remove only newly created unpublished chunks. This is sampled evidence, not a guarantee for every frame or for audio quality. JSON output contains per-clip scores and time ranges.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --source-password <password>
Source decryption and new-copy encryption password. Prefer environment or stdin to avoid shell history.
.TP
.B --source-password-env [name]
Read the password from the named environment variable, defaulting to TGLFS_WEBM_PASSWORD.
.TP
.B --source-password-stdin
Read the password from standard input.
.TP
.B --name <name>
Output name ending in .webm; defaults to the source stem plus .preview.webm.
.TP
.B --ffmpeg <path>
FFmpeg executable, default TGLFS_FFMPEG or ffmpeg.
.TP
.B --ffprobe <path>
ffprobe executable, default TGLFS_FFPROBE or ffprobe beside FFmpeg.
.TP
.B --no-interactive
Disable password prompts.
.TP
.B --json
Print a machine-readable result and quality report without prompting.
.SH RECOVERY
Cancellation and failure keep the original. If Telegram may have accepted the final card but its response was lost, new chunks are retained as well. Refresh or inspect Saved Messages before retrying, so a possibly published copy remains readable.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
tglfs convert-webm <ufid> --source-password-env --json
tglfs convert-webm <ufid> --name lecture.preview.webm
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tglfs (1),
.BR tglfs-make-previewable (1)
